###Question: Recognised causes of Secondary Polycythaemia include:
###Options:
A.  a.	Polycythaemia Rubra Vera;
B.  b.	High Altitude;
C.  c.	Hepatocellular Carcinoma;
D.  d.	Status Asthmaticus;
E.  e.	Congenital Cyanotic heart disease.

###Answer:

outputs:
 B. High Altitude; C. Hepatocellular Carcinoma; E. Congenital Cyanotic heart disease.

###Rationale: Secondary polycythaemia is a condition where there is an increase in the number of red blood cells due to external factors. High altitude (B) is a recognised cause because the lower oxygen levels at high altitudes stimulate the production of erythropoietin, which in turn increases red blood cell production. Hepatocellular carcinoma (C) can cause secondary polycythaemia due to the production of erythropoietin by the tumour. Congenital cyanotic heart disease (E) can lead to secondary polycythaemia as a compensatory mechanism for chronic hypoxia. Polycythaemia Rubra Vera (A) is a primary polycythaemia, not secondary. Status Asthmaticus (D) does not cause secondary polycythaemia.

###Question: Which of the following is not a recognised cause of Secondary Polycythaemia?
###Options:
A.  a.	Polycythaemia Rubra Vera;
B.  b.	High Altitude;
C.  c.	Hepatocellular Carcinoma;
D.  d.	Status Asthmaticus;
E.  e.	Congenital Cyanotic heart disease.

###Answer: A. Polycythaemia Rubra Vera; D. Status Asthmaticus.

###Rationale: Polycythaemia Rubra Vera (A) is a primary polycythaemia, not secondary. It is a myeloproliferative disorder where there is an increase in red blood cells due to a problem in the bone marrow, not due to external factors. Status Asthmaticus (D) does not cause secondary polycythaemia. High Altitude (B), Hepatocellular Carcinoma (C), and Congenital Cyanotic heart disease (E) are all recognised causes of secondary polycythaemia.

###Question: Which of the following is a recognised cause of Secondary Polycythaemia?
###Options:
A.  a	Polycythaemia Rubra Vera;
B.  b	High Altitude;
C.  c	Hepatocellular Carcinoma;
D.  d	Status Asthmaticus;
E.  e	Congenital Cyanotic heart disease.

###Answer: B. High Altitude;

