 C.  c.	Preceeding infection is usual in an adult;

###Rationale: Idiopathic Thrombocytopaenic Purpura (ITP) is an autoimmune disorder characterized by a low platelet count, leading to easy bruising and bleeding. The onset of ITP can be acute or chronic. In adults, ITP often follows a viral infection, which is thought to trigger the immune system to produce antibodies that mistakenly target and destroy platelets. This preceding infection is a common feature in adult cases of ITP, making option C the correct answer.

Option A is incorrect because ITP can have either an insidious or sudden onset, depending on whether it is chronic or acute, respectively. Option B is incorrect because spontaneous remission is more common in children than in adults with ITP. Option D is incorrect because the chronic form of ITP is more common in adults, and there is no strong gender predilection in the chronic type.

