###Answer:

outputs:
 B.  b.	Have Anti-A and Anti-B immunoglobulins in their sera;

###Explanation: Group O individuals have neither A nor B antigens on the surface of their red blood cells. However, they have both Anti-A and Anti-B antibodies (immunoglobulins) in their plasma. This is why they are considered universal donors for red blood cells, as their blood can be transfused to individuals of any ABO blood group without causing an immune reaction. The presence of these antibodies is a natural defense mechanism to protect against foreign A or B antigens that might be introduced into the body through blood transfusion or during pregnancy.

