###Question: In the context of ABO blood group system and Rh factor, which of the following statements is correct for a patient with blood type O negative (O-) who requires a blood transfusion?
###Options:
A.  a.	They can receive blood from any donor regardless of ABO or Rh status;
B.  b.	They can only receive blood from O negative donors;
C.  c.	They can receive blood from O positive donors but not from A, B, or AB donors;
D.  d.	They can receive blood from any Rh negative donor regardless of ABO status.

###Answer: B.  b.	They can only receive blood from O negative donors;

###Explanation: A patient with blood type O negative (O-) has neither A nor B antigens on their red blood cells and also lacks the Rh antigen (D antigen). This means they have both Anti-A and Anti-B antibodies in their plasma, as well as Anti-D antibodies if they have been previously sensitized to Rh-positive blood. Therefore, they can only receive blood from donors who also lack A, B, and Rh antigens to prevent an immune reaction. The only blood type that meets these criteria is O negative. Receiving blood from any other ABO or Rh-positive donor could lead to a hemolytic transfusion reaction, where the recipient's immune system attacks the transfused red blood cells, leading to potentially life-threatening complications.

